  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nModels show a broad upper level trof deepening over our region\nover the next 24-36 hours as an upper level high becomes more \nentrenched over the southwestern U.S.  At the surface, high\npressure off the southeast coast, along with a surface boundary\nto our north will result in our variable winds becoming\nsoutherly tonight into Friday, helping to increase low level\nmoisture across our region. Impulses moving along the upper \ntrof will continue on and off chances of showers and some \nthunderstorms tonight through Saturday. The best chances \n(40-60%) late tonight and Friday will be across southeast \nMissouri, southern Illinois and southwest Indiana, with more \nuniform 20-30% chances across the entire region Friday night \ninto Saturday. Overall rainfall amounts will range from less \nthan a tenth of an inch to a half inch, with localized higher \namounts with thunderstorms.\n\nBy late in the weekend and continuing into early next week, the\nsouthwest upper ridge will migrate a little farther east,\nresulting in dry conditions for our region, while southerly\nflow at the surface will help increase temperatures and\nhumidity. High temperatures in the Sunday to Tuesday time frame\nwill reach the 90 to 95 degree range, and with dew points in the\nlower and middle 70s, afternoon heat indices will climb into the\n100 to around 105 degree range. By mid week, the upper ridge \nwill be breaking down as an upper low over eastern Canada puts \nthe middle Mississippi and lower Ohio valley regions into more \nof a northwest flow pattern. This will put temperatures into a \ndownward trend along with an unsettled weather pattern. Chances\nof showers and thunderstorms return as early as Tuesday night, \nwith chances increasing and lingering through at least Thursday.",
